{'user': [{'id': 1, 'text': 'Hi '}, {'id': 2, 'text': "I have a really bad headache and don't feel well"}, {'id': 3, 'text': "I was at the cinema when it happened and the bright light on the screen started to hurt my eyes. My neck is a bit stiff as well  and it's hard to look down"}, {'id': 4, 'text': 'My friend, who I was with did remind me that I had drunk a bit too much earlier and my words were slurred'}, {'id': 5, 'text': "No, I was just looking for advice for the symptoms I have. I don't know if this would have anything to do with it but I did have Covid 19 about a year ago, which was pretty bad but I am over it, so maybe it was just too much alcohol h"}], 'bot': [{'id': 1, 'text': "Hello! I'm an AI assistant chatbot trained to help you with any queries you might have. I can provide information on a wide range of topics, offer explanations, assist with tasks, and engage in fun and interesting conversations. Just let me know what you need, and I'll do my best to assist!", 'score': 50}, {'id': 2, 'text': "I'm sorry to hear that you have a bad headache and are feeling unwell. Headaches are classified as either primary or secondary. A primary headache means that the headache is the main medical problem, although other factors may be identified. Other contributing factors include medicines, dehydration, or hormone changes. A secondary headache is related to an underlying medical condition, such as a neck injury, eye problems, jaw, teeth, or sinus infection. If you have a sudden, new, and severe headache or a headache with a fever, shortness of breath, stiff neck, or rash, seek medical care right away. Also, seek medical care if your headache is associated with neurological symptoms, such as a sudden loss of balance or falling, numbness or tingling, speech difficulties, personality changes/inappropriate behavior, or vision changes (blurry vision, double vision, or blind spots).", 'score': 50}, {'id': 3, 'text': "The bright light at the cinema may have hurt your eyes